---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="HAR Journal — Notes on coding agent infrastructure" bodyClass="blog-page" canonicalPath="/blog">
  <Header active="blog" solid />
  <main>
<section class="blog-hero blueprint-surface">
      <div class="container blog-hero-grid">
        <div class="reveal">
          <div class="eyebrow"><span class="eyebrow-dot"></span> The HAR journal</div>
          <h1>Notes from the <span class="ink-underline">factory floor.</span></h1>
          <p>Architecture, field notes, and practical patterns for building reliable coding-agent workflows.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="blog-hero-aside reveal reveal-delay-1">
          <span>Follow the build</span>
          <p>Occasional essays and release notes. No growth-hacking newsletter machinery.</p>
          <form
            class="newsletter-form"
            data-newsletter-form
            action="https://api.web3forms.com/submit"
            method="POST"
          >
            <input type="hidden" name="access_key" value="9650ee0d-81c2-4edb-9cab-69920938f8ab" />
            <input type="hidden" name="subject" value="HAR Journal newsletter signup" />
            <input type="hidden" name="message" value="Newsletter subscription from harproject.cloud/blog" />
            <input type="checkbox" name="botcheck" class="hidden" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" />
            <label class="sr-only" for="newsletter-email">Email</label>
            <input id="newsletter-email" name="email" type="email" placeholder="you@company.com" required />
            <button type="submit">Subscribe</button>
          </form>
          <small data-newsletter-status aria-live="polite">Or follow the <a href="/rss.xml">RSS feed</a>.</small>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section blog-feature-section">
      <div class="container blog-filter-row reveal"><div class="blog-filters"><button class="active">All</button><button>Architecture</button><button>Workflow</button><button>Releases</button><button>Field notes</button></div><span>7 essays</span></div>
      <div class="container featured-post reveal">
        <a class="featured-post-art blueprint-dark" href="/blog/why-coding-agents-need-a-harness">
          <div class="featured-grid-mark"></div>
          <div class="featured-node fn1"><span>01</span><strong>Agent</strong></div>
          <div class="featured-node fn2"><span>02</span><strong>Harness</strong></div>
          <div class="featured-node fn3"><span>03</span><strong>Evidence</strong></div>
          <svg viewBox="0 0 900 520" preserveAspectRatio="none"><path d="M210 265H360M540 265H690" /></svg>
          <div class="featured-stamp">FOUNDATION / 001</div>
        </a>
        <div class="featured-post-copy">
          <div class="article-meta"><span>Architecture</span><time>July 18, 2026 · 8 min</time></div>
          <h2><a href="/blog/why-coding-agents-need-a-harness">Why coding agents need a harness, not another prompt.</a></h2>
          <p>Coding models can edit files. Reliable software work requires a surrounding system that can isolate, launch, verify, and explain what happened.</p>
          <a class="text-link" href="/blog/why-coding-agents-need-a-harness">Read the essay <span>→</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section blog-list-section">
      <div class="container post-grid">
        <article class="post-card reveal"><a class="post-visual post-visual-file" href="/blog/why-coding-agents-need-a-harness"><span>.har/</span><small>contract</small></a><div class="article-meta"><span>Design notes</span><time>5 min</time></div><h3><a href="/blog/why-coding-agents-need-a-harness">The repository should own the agent contract.</a></h3><p>Portable infrastructure starts by keeping the operating knowledge beside the source code.</p><div class="post-footer"><time>July 11, 2026</time><a href="/blog/why-coding-agents-need-a-harness">Read →</a></div></article>
        <article class="post-card reveal reveal-delay-1"><a class="post-visual post-visual-lines" href="/blog/why-coding-agents-need-a-harness"><i></i><i></i><i></i><b>verify --full</b></a><div class="article-meta"><span>Workflow</span><time>6 min</time></div><h3><a href="/blog/why-coding-agents-need-a-harness">Evidence-first development for autonomous agents.</a></h3><p>Why a green check should carry logs, artifacts, timing, and an exact tree hash.</p><div class="post-footer"><time>July 4, 2026</time><a href="/blog/why-coding-agents-need-a-harness">Read →</a></div></article>
        <article class="post-card reveal reveal-delay-2"><a class="post-visual post-visual-number" href="/blog/why-coding-agents-need-a-harness"><span>0.14</span><small>release</small></a><div class="article-meta"><span>Release</span><time>3 min</time></div><h3><a href="/blog/why-coding-agents-need-a-harness">Mission Control, generic stages, and cleaner handoffs.</a></h3><p>A tour of the latest improvements across the CLI, MCP surface, and dashboard.</p><div class="post-footer"><time>June 28, 2026</time><a href="/blog/why-coding-agents-need-a-harness">Read →</a></div></article>
        <article class="post-card reveal"><a class="post-visual post-visual-terminal" href="/blog/why-coding-agents-need-a-harness"><code>$ har env launch 2<br /><span>✓ worktree ready</span><br /><span>✓ services running</span></code></a><div class="article-meta"><span>Field notes</span><time>7 min</time></div><h3><a href="/blog/why-coding-agents-need-a-harness">What breaks when three agents share one checkout?</a></h3><p>A practical look at process collisions, dirty state, ports, and unreviewable changes.</p><div class="post-footer"><time>June 20, 2026</time><a href="/blog/why-coding-agents-need-a-harness">Read →</a></div></article>
        <article class="post-card reveal reveal-delay-1"><a class="post-visual post-visual-slots" href="/blog/why-coding-agents-need-a-harness"><span>main</span><i></i><span>01</span><span>02</span><span>03</span></a><div class="article-meta"><span>Architecture</span><time>9 min</time></div><h3><a href="/blog/why-coding-agents-need-a-harness">Worktrees are necessary. They are not the whole harness.</a></h3><p>Isolation also means services, environment, databases, lifecycle, and verification.</p><div class="post-footer"><time>June 12, 2026</time><a href="/blog/why-coding-agents-need-a-harness">Read →</a></div></article>
        <article class="post-card reveal reveal-delay-2"><a class="post-visual post-visual-mcp" href="/blog/why-coding-agents-need-a-harness"><span>MCP</span><small>generic surface</small></a><div class="article-meta"><span>Design notes</span><time>5 min</time></div><h3><a href="/blog/why-coding-agents-need-a-harness">A small MCP surface is a feature.</a></h3><p>Stable generic stages beat a growing collection of framework-specific agent tools.</p><div class="post-footer"><time>June 5, 2026</time><a href="/blog/why-coding-agents-need-a-harness">Read →</a></div></article>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>
